WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.266 --> 00:00:01.901
-Welcome back, everybody. We are
talking to Senator Cory Booker

00:00:01.901 --> 00:00:04.471 size:84% position:42%
The President names you
specifically and says

00:00:04.471 --> 00:00:08.008
you are trying to destroy
the American dream by supporting

00:00:08.008 --> 00:00:10.443 align:start size:94% position:6%
affordable housing
in the suburbs.

00:00:10.443 --> 00:00:11.611 line:92%
You grew up in the suburbs

00:00:11.611 --> 00:00:16.016
Can you speak to the sort of
blatant racism behind this

00:00:16.016 --> 00:00:19.319 align:start size:94% position:6%
both where he stands on
the issue and why he keeps

00:00:19.319 --> 00:00:20.720 line:92%
bringing your name into it

00:00:20.720 --> 00:00:22.455 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Look, as one of my close
friends said to me,

00:00:22.455 --> 00:00:24.958
the fact that the Presiden
brought you up multiple time

00:00:24.958 --> 00:00:28.361 align:start size:94% position:6%
in tweets and rallies
should be a sign

00:00:28.361 --> 00:00:30.397 align:start size:94% position:6%
that you're actually
doing something.

00:00:30.397 --> 00:00:31.898 align:start size:94% position:6%
But speaking to him
is not what --

00:00:31.898 --> 00:00:32.966 align:start size:94% position:6%
frankly, if I can
answer your question more

00:00:32.966 --> 00:00:36.970
by speaking to us, as a country,
we are this phenomenal natio

00:00:36.970 --> 00:00:41.508
with reservoirs of love an
decency that are always ther

00:00:41.508 --> 00:00:43.343 align:start size:94% position:6%
but we don't always
draw down upon them.

00:00:43.343 --> 00:00:50.550
And this is a president who ha
so, I think -- been such poiso

00:00:50.550 --> 00:00:52.585 align:start size:94% position:6%
to the spirit and the energy
that's in our country.

00:00:52.585 --> 00:00:55.422
And I know of no other way
to do that than to reject it

00:00:55.422 --> 00:00:58.558
And not to reject it with
just, like, more bile.

00:00:58.558 --> 00:01:01.261 align:start size:94% position:6%
I still remember,
in one of my town halls

00:01:01.261 --> 00:01:02.862 align:start size:94% position:6%
when I was running for
president, a guy comes up to m

00:01:02.862 --> 00:01:05.698
and says, "Dude, I want you to
punch Donald Trump in the face."

00:01:05.698 --> 00:01:07.067 align:start size:94% position:6%
And I looked at him
and I laughed.

00:01:07.067 --> 00:01:08.301 align:start size:94% position:6%
And I said,
"Dude, that's a felony."

00:01:08.301 --> 00:01:11.838
We cannot beat that energy b
bringing more of that energy

00:01:11.838 --> 00:01:14.207 size:84% position:42%
That's what he wants.
He wants us to get down

00:01:14.207 --> 00:01:15.542 size:84% position:42% line:92%
and fight in the gutter

00:01:15.542 --> 00:01:17.377 align:start size:94% position:6%
and in a way
that none of us win.

00:01:17.377 --> 00:01:21.314 align:start size:94% position:6%
This is a time when we see
his kind of racism and bigotry

00:01:21.314 --> 00:01:23.416 align:start size:94% position:6%
We see his blatant
fearmongering,

00:01:23.416 --> 00:01:30.123 align:start size:94% position:6%
trying to prey upon the
insecurities or latent strains

00:01:30.123 --> 00:01:31.624 line:92%
of negativity in our society

00:01:31.624 --> 00:01:33.993 align:start size:94% position:6%
We have to reject it
and reject him.

00:01:33.993 --> 00:01:35.628 line:92%
I don't care what your party is.

00:01:35.628 --> 00:01:37.931 align:start size:94% position:6%
Both parties,
Republicans and Democrats,

00:01:37.931 --> 00:01:38.765 size:84% position:42% line:92%
we're better than this.

00:01:38.765 --> 00:01:41.101
We should be calling peopl
to the best of our angels,

00:01:41.101 --> 00:01:44.871 align:start size:94% position:6%
the highest of our aspirations
to the best of our virtues

00:01:44.871 --> 00:01:46.940 size:75% position:38%
and not what he does
on a regular basis,

00:01:46.940 --> 00:01:53.413
which is demonstrate vile,
dehumanizing, debasing behavior.

00:01:53.413 --> 00:01:56.116 align:start size:94% position:6%
It's just wrong
and, God, I just --

00:01:56.116 --> 00:01:57.717 align:start size:94% position:6%
I don't want to
just win this election

00:01:57.717 --> 00:01:59.152 align:start size:94% position:6%
for some kind of
partisan reason.

00:01:59.152 --> 00:02:04.424 align:start size:94% position:6%
I want this to be a national
rejection of Trumpism,

00:02:04.424 --> 00:02:08.361 align:start size:94% position:6%
of all that he has brought
into our public spaces.

00:02:08.361 --> 00:02:10.797 align:start size:94% position:6%
And I want us to see,
at the end of this election,

00:02:10.797 --> 00:02:13.566 align:start size:94% position:6%
not just one candidate
being elevated but all of us

00:02:13.566 --> 00:02:16.136 size:75% position:38%
committing ourselves
to having a revival

00:02:16.136 --> 00:02:17.504 line:92%
in this country of civic grace

00:02:17.504 --> 00:02:22.075
-You had some spirited moments
with former Vice President Biden

00:02:22.075 --> 00:02:23.176 line:92%
during the Democratic debate

00:02:23.176 --> 00:02:26.379 align:start size:94% position:6%
but some amicable moments
during the commercial breaks

00:02:26.379 --> 00:02:28.848 align:start size:94% position:6%
Is that right?
-Yeah.

00:02:28.848 --> 00:02:31.284 line:92%
I have to say, I knew Joe Biden.

00:02:31.284 --> 00:02:32.819 align:start size:94% position:6%
He swore me into the job
that I had.

00:02:32.819 --> 00:02:36.789
But I really got to know him i
the way you do with, you know,

00:02:36.789 --> 00:02:40.026
the rival kids you played with
in high school, for example.

00:02:40.026 --> 00:02:43.363 align:start size:94% position:6%
And I am just a big believer
that contests like that

00:02:43.363 --> 00:02:45.498 align:start size:94% position:6%
don't build character,
they reveal it.

00:02:45.498 --> 00:02:47.066 line:92%
And by the end of the campaign

00:02:47.066 --> 00:02:50.270
I was thoroughly disarmed by him
and admired him.

00:02:50.270 --> 00:02:51.304 line:92%
And I remember a tough moment.

00:02:51.304 --> 00:02:53.740 size:84% position:42%
He had said something
that I found offensive.

00:02:53.740 --> 00:02:58.511 align:start size:94% position:6%
And I still remember,
I was on a major news network,

00:02:58.511 --> 00:02:59.679 line:92%
talking about it from my heart

00:02:59.679 --> 00:03:02.282 align:start size:94% position:6%
and speaking about
why it was so hurtful to me.

00:03:02.282 --> 00:03:04.450 align:start size:94% position:6%
And then something
that has never happened to m

00:03:04.450 --> 00:03:07.720 size:88% position:44%
before in public life --
I got off that station

00:03:07.720 --> 00:03:10.657 align:start size:94% position:6%
and I get a phone call
from Joe Biden.

00:03:10.657 --> 00:03:11.491 size:84% position:42% line:92%
And he doesn't call me.

00:03:11.491 --> 00:03:15.195
It's not one of these puffery,
political-speak kind of calls.

00:03:15.195 --> 00:03:16.963 align:start size:94% position:6%
He called me
with a sense of vulnerabilit

00:03:16.963 --> 00:03:22.035
and saying to me, really, that
he doesn't always get it right

00:03:22.035 --> 00:03:25.138 align:start size:94% position:6%
and that he is still learnin
and he is still growing

00:03:25.138 --> 00:03:27.440 align:start size:94% position:6%
and he really
wanted to understand.

00:03:27.440 --> 00:03:29.375 align:start size:94% position:6%
And, you know,
Trump has this way of

00:03:29.375 --> 00:03:31.611 align:start size:94% position:6%
never being wrong,
never apologizing.

00:03:31.611 --> 00:03:33.846 align:start size:94% position:6%
Biden has this way of saying
"You know what?

00:03:33.846 --> 00:03:35.882 line:92%
None of us are beyond sticking

00:03:35.882 --> 00:03:37.317 align:start size:94% position:6%
our foot in our mouth,
making mistakes.

00:03:37.317 --> 00:03:40.853
But maybe, if we own up to them,
we can all learn together,

00:03:40.853 --> 00:03:43.089 align:start size:94% position:6%
all grow together,
and heal together."

00:03:43.089 --> 00:03:45.892 align:start size:94% position:6%
And so,
by the end of the campaign

00:03:45.892 --> 00:03:49.128
I considered him a friend
and someone that I admired

00:03:49.128 --> 00:03:52.899
and now someone I very muc
want, especially at this tim

00:03:52.899 --> 00:03:55.768 align:start size:94% position:6%
where we need healing,
I want him to be my president.

00:03:55.768 --> 00:03:59.772 align:start size:94% position:6%
-I asked your colleague Senato
Klobuchar this on Monday.

00:03:59.772 --> 00:04:02.575 align:start size:94% position:6%
What are your plans
for election night?

00:04:02.575 --> 00:04:04.611 line:92%
Where are you going to watch it?

00:04:04.611 --> 00:04:06.045 size:81% position:41% line:92%
How you going to feel?

00:04:06.045 --> 00:04:11.918
-So, my hope is just to be
relaxed while sitting next t

00:04:11.918 --> 00:04:18.691
Rosario on a couch somewhere
and eating junk food vegan

00:04:18.691 --> 00:04:22.295
And, God willing, watching
an election that I hope --

00:04:22.295 --> 00:04:23.997 align:start size:94% position:6%
we should all
have the expectation

00:04:23.997 --> 00:04:26.332 align:start size:94% position:6%
that it won't be
decided that night.

00:04:26.332 --> 00:04:27.800 align:start size:94% position:6%
But I have a lot of hope
that night

00:04:27.800 --> 00:04:29.569 align:start size:94% position:6%
that there'll be
a lot to celebrate.

00:04:29.569 --> 00:04:31.771 line:92%
And also, you know, the Senate

00:04:31.771 --> 00:04:34.941
I mean, Mitch McConnell, who
calls himself the Grim Reaper,

00:04:34.941 --> 00:04:40.179
I'm really hoping that from Iowa
to Texas to South Carolina

00:04:40.179 --> 00:04:44.183
that we win a lot of Senat
seats that people didn't expect.

00:04:44.183 --> 00:04:45.351 size:88% position:44% line:92%
Surprise a lot of people

00:04:45.351 --> 00:04:48.721 align:start size:94% position:6%
and give a further repudiation
of Trumpism.

00:04:48.721 --> 00:04:49.889 line:92%
-Alright. All the best to you.

00:04:49.889 --> 00:04:51.758 align:start size:94% position:6%
Thank you so much for making
time for us, Senator.

00:04:51.758 --> 00:04:52.759 align:start size:94% position:6%
Really appreciate it.
-Hey.

00:04:52.759 --> 00:04:54.193 align:start size:94% position:6%
I just want to tell you
something because

00:04:54.193 --> 00:04:57.397 line:92%
a lot of times, people think o

00:04:57.397 --> 00:04:59.065 align:start size:94% position:6%
entertainment or art
as entertainment.

00:04:59.065 --> 00:05:02.201
And I just want to let you kno
that during this time of Trump

00:05:02.201 --> 00:05:03.970 size:88% position:44%
you've just been one of
those folks that I think

00:05:03.970 --> 00:05:06.839 size:88% position:44%
has given a master class
in showing that satire

00:05:06.839 --> 00:05:09.942 align:start size:94% position:6%
is essential to a democracy,
the ability not only to

00:05:09.942 --> 00:05:14.380
criticize power but, really,
call it into question in a way

00:05:14.380 --> 00:05:17.083
that stimulates people's
understanding and action.

00:05:17.083 --> 00:05:21.254
Art is essential to a democracy,
especially in a time of trial.

00:05:21.254 --> 00:05:24.390
And you've just done a wonderful
job and have given me

00:05:24.390 --> 00:05:28.928
not only a lot of laughter but
even sustained and nurtured me

00:05:28.928 --> 00:05:31.464 align:start size:94% position:6%
and nourished me in a time tha
I really needed it.

00:05:31.464 --> 00:05:33.566 align:start size:94% position:6%
-I'm so glad you said that
in front of my crew,

00:05:33.566 --> 00:05:38.571 align:start size:94% position:6%
who do not respect me at all
[ Laughter ]

